{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"c0fed31b-f4fb-4f95-9907-c6e797902c5d","title":"Pythagorean theorem with a visual proof"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c0fed31b-f4fb-4f95-9907-c6e797902c5d","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Start with a square whose side is a plus b. We will fill it with four identical right triangles.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Pythagorean theorem"},{"op":"figure","id":"proof","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.7,5.7],"y":[-0.8,5.5],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"outer","points":[[0,0],[5,0],[5,5],[0,5]],"closed":true},{"kind":"measure","from":[0,-0.35],"to":[3,-0.35],"label":"a"},{"kind":"measure","from":[3,-0.35],"to":[5,-0.35],"label":"b"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"whole","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"\\text{Whole area}=(a+b)^2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c0fed31b-f4fb-4f95-9907-c6e797902c5d","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Each triangle has perpendicular legs a and b. Four copies fit around the edges, with their hypotenuses facing inward.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"triangles","on":"proof","marks":[{"kind":"group","origin":[2.5,2.5],"rotate":90,"repeat":4,"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0,0],[3,0],[0,2]],"closed":true,"fill":true}]},{"kind":"point","id":"p","at":[3,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"q","at":[5,3]},{"kind":"point","id":"r","at":[2,5]},{"kind":"point","id":"s","at":[0,2]},{"kind":"angle","at":[0,0],"from":[3,0],"to":[0,2],"r":0.08}]},{"op":"equation","id":"triangle_area","anchor":{"el":"whole","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"\\text{Four triangles: }4\\left(\\frac{ab}{2}\\right)=2ab"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c0fed31b-f4fb-4f95-9907-c6e797902c5d","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The central shape has four sides of length c. Its corners are right angles because the triangles’ two acute angles sum to ninety degrees.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"center","on":"proof","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"inner","points":[{"at":"p"},{"at":"q"},{"at":"r"},{"at":"s"}],"closed":true},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"p"},"to":{"at":"q"},"label":"c"},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"q"},"from":{"at":"r"},"to":{"at":"p"},"r":0.08},{"kind":"text","at":[2.5,2.5],"text":"c²","size":"large"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"balance","anchor":{"el":"triangle_area","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"(a+b)^2=2ab+c^2"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Why is the central region a square rather than just a rhombus?","expected_gist":"Equal sides c and four right angles"}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c0fed31b-f4fb-4f95-9907-c6e797902c5d","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Expand the outer square’s area and cancel the triangle area. What remains proves that a squared plus b squared equals c squared.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"expand","anchor":{"el":"balance","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"a^2+2ab+b^2=2ab+c^2"},{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"expand","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"\\boxed{a^2+b^2=c^2}"},{"op":"figure","id":"legs","on":"proof","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":[0,0],"to":{"at":"p"},"label":"a"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"s"},"to":[0,0],"label":"b"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
